{
  "title": "I can swim: can and can't",
  "kind": "grammar",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "estMinutes": 8,
  "intro": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Form"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": true,
      "rows": [
        [
          "positive",
          "negative",
          "question"
        ],
        [
          "I / you / he / she / we / they can swim.",
          "… can't (cannot) swim.",
          "Can you swim?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": true,
      "rows": [
        [
          "question",
          "short answers"
        ],
        [
          "Can you drive?",
          "Yes, I can. / No, I can't."
        ],
        [
          "Can she cook?",
          "Yes, she can. / No, she can't."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Rules"
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "can is special",
      "points": [
        "It is the same for every person: he can (not he cans).",
        "The next verb has no to and no -s: She can swim. (not can to swim / can swims)",
        "No do in questions: Can you swim? (not Do you can swim?)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Use"
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "We use can for",
      "points": [
        "ability: I can speak three languages.",
        "possibility: You can buy tickets online.",
        "asking for permission: Can I open the window? — Yes, you can."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "Pronunciation",
      "points": [
        "can is usually short /kən/: I can swim.",
        "can't is long /kɑːnt/: I can't swim."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Be careful!"
    },
    {
      "type": "mistake",
      "wrong": "She cans drive.",
      "right": "She can drive."
    },
    {
      "type": "mistake",
      "wrong": "I can to speak English.",
      "right": "I can speak English."
    },
    {
      "type": "mistake",
      "wrong": "Do you can cook?",
      "right": "Can you cook?"
    }
  ],
